# Where the Jaffle Shop corpus came from.
#
# The corpus was made in two steps, both on 2026-09-24: ONE run of jafgen wrote a full
# year of data, and derive/trim.py cut that year down to its first calendar quarter. The
# full-year files are not committed. They are archived as the asset
# jaffle-fullyear-jafgen-0.4.14.tar.gz (sha256 bd37c5d94a332ea93f6e24ce4f5b5f94b70f189f
# 4ec5e31e6f32b3333e15568b) of the release corpus/jaffle-fullyear-2026-09-24 of this
# repository. Their digests are below, and trim.py refuses any input that does not match
# them. derive/trim.SHA256SUMS pins the bytes trim.py writes, and derive/reproduce.sh
# checks the archive, runs trim.py and compares its output against those digests.
#
# THE ONE GENERATION RUN
#
# That run is the only time jafgen was used, and it must never be repeated as part of
# any automated path: not by a seed, a test, CI, a Makefile target or the tutorial.
# jafgen draws fresh UUIDs and unseeded random numbers on every run, so a second run
# produces different rows, and nothing could assert against it.
#
# How the run was made, exactly:
#
#   uv venv --python 3.12 venv            # an isolated venv; nothing installed globally
#   uv pip install --python venv/bin/python 'jafgen==0.4.14'
#   cd <empty dir> && venv/bin/jafgen 1 --pre raw
#   # jafgen writes ./jaffle-data/raw_*.csv; those seven files are the input to trim.py
#
#   Arguments   years=1 (the positional argument), --pre raw (the default prefix)
#   Started     2026-09-24T04:50:10Z; exit status 0
#   Host        macOS 27.0 arm64, uv 0.12.18, CPython 3.12.14
#   Installed   jafgen==0.4.14, faker==40.39.0, numpy==2.5.3, typer==0.27.2,
#               rich==15.0.0, annotated-doc==0.0.5, markdown-it-py==4.2.0, mdurl==0.1.2,
#               pygments==2.21.0, shellingham==1.5.4 (the complete `uv pip freeze`)
#   Wheel       jafgen-0.4.14-py3-none-any.whl, sha256
#               0f92684fb49050a56a4de5d8214b90365ff84ef6ae7c685abf899e0ad1918913 (PyPI)
#   Source      github.com/dbt-labs/jaffle-shop-generator, tag v0.4.14, commit
#               9384961105e3de9296c61b235155f3240fab1140; its pyproject.toml declares
#               version 0.4.14
#
# WHY ONE YEAR, THEN ONE QUARTER
#
# years=1 is the smallest run the CLI accepts that writes data: the argument is a whole
# number of years, the customer base per store is fixed in jafgen's code, and, reading
# that code, a zero-year run simulates no days and has no rows to write. A year was
# still too large for a corpus that ships in a public quickstart, so it was trimmed.
#
# The full-year files, as the run wrote them (rows are data lines, header excluded,
# counted with Python's csv module):
#
#   raw_customers.csv       946 rows        49,511 bytes
#   raw_items.csv       107,853 rows     8,951,816 bytes
#   raw_orders.csv       72,610 rows    10,432,080 bytes
#   raw_products.csv         10 rows           923 bytes
#   raw_stores.csv            6 rows           477 bytes
#   raw_supplies.csv         65 rows         2,655 bytes
#   raw_tweets.csv       22,476 rows     4,038,262 bytes
#
# and their SHA-256 digests, the SHA256SUMS of the full year:
#
#   d0633d42103841f51dfb05474bdc66b9962b3368f134bbf4d1dc2e15b9012292  raw_customers.csv
#   1ddb9ccd5bfdbd1587bccf3b06c5e4c96d0a8647771b02637af58d9b1adf26f8  raw_items.csv
#   19b051ac14ce299fc2b8d6a0088d1c9d88d0bd98e4d75432966666f4a219e5f5  raw_orders.csv
#   01825b18e182090b6770cee6aaf7bd52d5781a0c74bb1ff2872aac4b47809f0d  raw_products.csv
#   a83ac006ab8cab7cd7722d0cfeab87b01d7a9caa4cc373eb63de5e13764d5683  raw_stores.csv
#   309a2d1cfd330da2b493b411d2fe5e0aef1b463ed0281760691793f8b423bde1  raw_supplies.csv
#   fb8f1adfe8bda55d753b5a9c01adde1fa94aa88d9a3b46b12957021e702b8861  raw_tweets.csv
#
# THE TRIM
#
#   python3 derive/trim.py <full-year dir> <empty dir>    # Python standard library only
#
# trim.py's own SHA-256 is TRIM_SHA256 below; its docstring states the rule, and in
# short it is:
#
#   * the window is the half-open interval [2018-09-01T08:20:00, 2018-12-01T00:00:00) on
#     raw_orders.ordered_at: from the earliest order to midnight on the first day of the
#     third calendar month after it, so September, October and November 2018;
#   * raw_orders keeps the orders in the window, raw_items the items of those orders,
#     and raw_tweets the tweets whose tweeted_at lies in the same window;
#   * raw_customers keeps exactly the customers those orders name. The full year has no
#     customer without an order, and the quarter keeps that true: a snapshot taken at the
#     end of the window holds no customer whose first order comes after it, so a later
#     delta adds a new customer as a new row. Every kept tweet's user is one of them;
#   * raw_stores, raw_products and raw_supplies are jafgen's fixed reference tables,
#     written whole on every run, and pass through byte for byte. Only jafgen's
#     Philadelphia store trades in the window; the other five stores open later, as the
#     full year's Chicago, San Francisco, New Orleans and Los Angeles already did. Those
#     are jafgen's names, which step 4 below replaces: the published corpus holds none.
#
# Kept rows are the full year's bytes, in its order, CRLF line endings included. Every
# reference a kept row makes resolves to a kept row. trim.py is deterministic: two runs
# on the full-year files wrote identical files. The trimmed corpus is 2,214,285 bytes:
#
#   raw_customers.csv       232 rows        12,097 bytes
#   raw_items.csv        10,025 rows       832,092 bytes
#   raw_orders.csv        6,837 rows       982,871 bytes
#   raw_products.csv         10 rows           923 bytes
#   raw_stores.csv            6 rows           477 bytes
#   raw_supplies.csv         65 rows         2,655 bytes
#   raw_tweets.csv        2,161 rows       383,170 bytes
#
# 19,336 rows in all. Their digests are in derive/trim.SHA256SUMS. The trim output is an
# intermediate: it is not committed, and the later steps below change its bytes.
#
# THE DERIVATION CHAIN
#
#   1. archive              jaffle-fullyear-jafgen-0.4.14.tar.gz, the one generation run
#   2. derive/trim.py       the first calendar quarter of orders and what they reference
#                           (this step; checked by derive/reproduce.sh)
#   3. derive/shift.py      moves every timestamp forward by 2,555 days (365 weeks) and gives
#                           the files their published names (this step;
#                           derive/shift.SHA256SUMS)
#   4. derive/localize.py   makes the corpus European: names, stores, VAT, menu, tweets
#                           (this step; data/, SHA256SUMS)
#   5. derive/addresses.py  adds a deterministic address per customer (this step; data/,
#                           SHA256SUMS)
#   6. data/ + SHA256SUMS   the pinned outputs of the chain (steps 4 and 5 write to them)
#   7. db/load.sql          the consumer of data/: loads it into PostgreSQL; writes no file
#   8. db/run2.sql          run 2: hand-authored changes to the loaded source; writes no file
#   9. expected/counts.yaml the expected results of runs 1 and 2, derived by hand from the
#                           target's history templates; nothing generates it
#
# Step 3 shifts stores.opened_at, orders.ordered_at and tweets.tweeted_at by exactly 2,555
# days, as whole days on the zone-less timestamps. 2,555 is a whole number of weeks, so
# every weekday and every wall-clock time is unchanged, daylight saving included. The
# first order, 2018-09-01T08:20:00, becomes 2025-08-30T08:20:00; the orders end on
# 2025-11-28; the first store opens on 2025-08-30 and the second on 2026-03-10. Nothing else
# changes except the published names: raw_items.csv becomes order_items.csv, the other raw_
# prefixes drop, and the header columns orders.customer and tweets.user_id become
# customer_id. shift.py's own SHA-256 is SHIFT_SHA256 below; its input is checked against
# trim.SHA256SUMS before it reads anything, and derive/shift.SHA256SUMS pins what it writes.
# Its output is an intermediate: it is not committed.
#
# Step 4 makes the corpus European, at jaffle/v3.0.0. derive/localize.py replaces every
# customer name, the store cities and tax rates, and the product and supply text that read
# as American or named a real brand or person; it carries the new product names into the
# tweets, and computes tax_paid and order_total again. A customer's name is drawn from two
# lists of common names and skips EXCLUDED_NAMES, the names a screen or search turned up. The
# people are invented, but a name can still match a real person's; the comment on that list
# says what was screened. Ids, timestamps, row counts, row order, SKUs, prices and subtotals
# do not change.
# Amounts are integer euro cents, subtotal excludes VAT, tax_rate is one flat VAT rate per
# store, tax_paid is exactly subtotal * tax_rate and order_total is subtotal + tax_paid; its
# docstring says why nothing rounds. Its input is checked against shift.SHA256SUMS before it
# reads anything, SHA256SUMS in this directory pins every file in data/, and
# derive/reproduce.sh requires its output to match both data/ and SHA256SUMS exactly. Its
# own SHA-256 is LOCALIZE_SHA256 below.
#
# Step 5 writes data/customer_addresses.csv (customer_id, street, postal_code, city,
# country): one synthetic Utrecht address per customer, in the order of customers.csv.
# jafgen wrote none of it. Every choice is a SHA-256 over a named salt and the customer id,
# so the file is the same on every run, Python version and PYTHONHASHSEED; the invented
# street names and the postal code parts are embedded in addresses.py. addresses.py checks
# customers.csv against SHA256SUMS before reading it, and reproduce.sh requires its output
# to equal the committed file. Its own SHA-256 is ADDRESSES_SHA256 below.
#
# Step 6 is complete when the pinned data lands; data/ and SHA256SUMS already cover steps 4
# and 5.
#
# Step 7 reads data/ and changes none of it. db/load.sql creates the schemas sales,
# catalog and marketing and their seven tables, with a comment on every schema, table and
# column; copies each file into its table (customers.csv into sales.customers,
# order_items.csv into sales.order_items, and so on), folds customer_addresses.csv into
# sales.customers' address columns, adds the primary keys and six foreign keys after the
# rows are in, and asserts each table's row count against the published files' counts: the
# trimmed counts above, which the shift and the renames leave unchanged, and 232
# addresses, one per customer. It runs in one transaction, so a failure leaves nothing
# behind. The files are read from a psql variable, datadir (default /data/jaffle); check
# them against SHA256SUMS first. load.sql refuses to run outside one transaction; in a
# postgres container, db/initdb.sh runs it from /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d in one.
# db/check_load.sql checks the loaded model: types, nullability, comments, keys and
# constraints, and row counts counted from the files themselves. CI (the load job) runs
# load.sql and the check against a digest-pinned PostgreSQL 17.
#
# Step 8 is not derived from anything: db/run2.sql is written by hand, against the loaded
# corpus, and jafgen plays no part in it. It is the stack's goldenset delta carried over to
# these names, with its dates moved by the same 2,555 days, addresses for its two new
# customers, and one added change: an existing customer, chosen by the rule its header
# states, moves to Rotterdam and places that store's first order on its opening day. It
# runs in its own transaction and changes the source only; the files in data/ stay as
# published. db/check_run2.sql checks what row counts cannot show: every order's amounts
# and VAT, run 2's prices, no order before its store opened, Rotterdam's first order, and
# that the mover's new address is in Rotterdam.
#
# Step 9, expected/counts.yaml, holds row counts per table for the source and for the
# warehouse's staging, history and bridge layers, after run 1 and after run 2, the job
# they expect (its layer, schema prefix, and each table's type, history mode and event-time
# column), and the history reveal: the moving customer's id and both addresses, and the
# deleted customer's id and address, each with the history and bridge_act rows expected
# after run 2. Its source counts and the reveal's source rows are checked by CI, against
# the database (db/source_counts.sql); its warehouse counts are derived from the target's
# history templates, and its header shows the arithmetic. The stack's engine-capture scenario
# asserts them against an engine run.
# expected/counts.py validates the file's structure and reads values from it, with the
# Python standard library only. CI (the load job) runs load.sql, the counts of run 1,
# run2.sql, its check and the counts of run 2, in that order.

